  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front progged to cont moving well offshore\ntoday. Thus deep layer nerly flow expected for the majority of\nENC. Have cut back on pops as a result, now only maintaining\npops south of Hwy 70. Best moisture will reside acrs the far\nswrn zones, where this area stands the best chance of receiving\nsome showers and perhaps a rumble of thunder this afternoon. \nHigh pres in control for Sunday means a drier fcst on tap, and\nhave cut back on pops as well. Next appreciable chance of rain\narrives Mon and Tue as yet another frontal boundary pushes\nsouthward.\n\nKey Message 2...Next week, a strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the \nsame time low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This will likely usher in stronger NE winds to the \nregion. Given it is 4-6 days out, there is still much \nuncertainty, as both wind speed and direction is dependent on \nthe placement, strength, and timing of the low. For now there is\ngood model consensus on at least higher waves 8+ ft at 8+ sec \nalong the Outer Banks mid- next week. This will be worth keeping\nan eye on, as a long fetch of strong winds could bring higher \nwave energy towards vulnerable portions of the Outer Banks. It \nis too soon to determine specifics at this point and trends will\nhave to be monitored in the coming days.",
